\label{ch:compiler-verification}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:verification-intro}

Compilers are among the most complex and widely used software systems. A bug in a compiler can silently introduce errors into every program it compiles, potentially affecting millions of users. Compiler bugs have caused security vulnerabilities, incorrect scientific computations, and subtle data corruption. Yet compilers are rarely verified: we trust them based on testing, which can never prove the absence of bugs.

\emph{Compiler verification} is the application of formal methods to prove that a compiler correctly translates every source program into an equivalent target program. A verified compiler comes with a mathematical proof that its output preserves the semantics of its input. This chapter covers the theory and practice of compiler verification, from translation validation to certified compilation and the landmark CompCert project.

\section{Why Verify Compilers?}\label{sec:why-verify}

\subsection{The Stakes}\label{sec:stakes}

Compilers occupy a unique position in the software stack: they are trusted by all the software they produce. If a compiler introduces a bug, that bug propagates to every compiled program. This makes compiler correctness especially important for:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Safety-critical systems}: avionics, medical devices, nuclear control systems, where incorrect code can cause loss of life.
  \item \textbf{Security-critical systems}: cryptographic implementations, operating system kernels, where incorrect code can introduce vulnerabilities.
  \item \textbf{Scientific computing}: simulations, numerical analysis, where incorrect code can invalidate research results.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Compiler Bugs in Practice}\label{sec:bugs-practice}

Compiler bugs are not rare:
\begin{itemize}
  \item GCC and LLVM each have hundreds of bug reports per year related to incorrect code generation.
  \item The ``Optimization Wall'' phenomenon: code works at low optimization levels but fails at higher levels due to optimization bugs.
  \item Security vulnerabilities: compiler bugs have been used to insert backdoors into compiled code (the Thompson attack, \cref{sec:thompson-attack}).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Testing Is Not Enough}\label{sec:testing-limits}

Testing can find bugs but cannot prove their absence:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The space of possible programs is infinite; testing can only sample it.
  \item Optimization bugs often manifest only for specific combinations of source patterns.
  \item Regressions can be introduced by changes to the optimizer.
\end{itemize}

Formal verification provides a stronger guarantee: a \emph{proof} that the compiler is correct for \emph{all} programs.

\section{Translation Validation}\label{sec:translation-validation}

\subsection{The Concept}\label{sec:tv-concept}

\begin{definition}[Translation Validation]\label{def:translation-validation}
\emph{Translation validation} is a technique that verifies the correctness of a compiler's output \emph{after} compilation. For each compilation, a validator checks that the target program is semantically equivalent to the source program.
\end{definition}

Unlike full compiler verification, translation validation does not prove the compiler correct in general. Instead, it validates each individual compilation.

\subsection{How Translation Validation Works}\label{sec:tv-how}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The compiler produces a target program $T$ from a source program $S$.
  \item A validator checks that $T$ is semantically equivalent to $S$.
  \item If the check succeeds, the compilation is accepted.
  \item If the check fails, the compilation is rejected (and the compiler bug is reported).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Validation Techniques}\label{sec:tv-techniques}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Simulation checking}: construct a simulation relation between the source and target semantics.
  \item \textbf{Equivalence checking}: use SMT solvers to check that the source and target compute the same function.
  \item \textbf{Proof-carrying code}: the compiler produces a proof of correctness along with the target code.
  \item \textbf{Abstract interpretation}: verify that the target program satisfies the same abstract properties as the source.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Advantages and Limitations}\label{sec:tv-pros-cons}

\textbf{Advantages}:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Does not require verifying the compiler itself.
  \item Can be applied to any compiler, including unverified ones.
  \item Catches optimization bugs that would otherwise go undetected.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Limitations}:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Adds overhead to each compilation.
  \item May produce false negatives (rejecting correct compilations).
  \item Does not prevent the compiler from crashing or producing malformed output.
  \item Validation itself must be trusted (it must be correct).
\end{itemize}

\section{Certified Compilation}\label{sec:certified-compilation}

\subsection{The Concept}\label{sec:certified-concept}

\begin{definition}[Certified Compilation]\label{def:certified-compilation}
\emph{Certified compilation} is a compilation strategy where the compiler produces, along with the target code, a \emph{certificate} (proof) that the target code correctly implements the source program's semantics.
\end{definition}

The certificate can be checked by a small, trusted proof checker. If the checker accepts the certificate, the user can trust the compiled code without trusting the compiler.

\subsection{Proof Architecture}\label{sec:proof-architecture}

The proof architecture typically involves:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A formal semantics for the source language.
  \item A formal semantics for the target language.
  \item A specification of the compilation relation: $S \leadsto T$ means ``$T$ correctly implements $S$.''
  \item A proof that the compiler establishes this relation.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Semantic Preservation}\label{sec:semantic-preservation}

The key theorem is \emph{semantic preservation}:

\begin{theorem}[Semantic Preservation]\label{thm:semantic-preservation}
If $\text{compile}(S) = T$, then for all observable behaviors $b$:
\[
  S \Downarrow b \iff T \Downarrow b
\]
That is, the source and target programs have the same observable behaviors.
\end{theorem}

The notion of ``observable behavior'' must be carefully defined. Typically, it includes:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Input/output (reads from and writes to volatile memory, system calls).
  \item Termination (does the program halt?).
  \item Non-termination (does the program diverge?).
  \item Undefined behavior (does the program go wrong?).
\end{itemize}

\section{CompCert: A Verified Compiler}\label{sec:compcert}

\subsection{Overview}\label{sec:compcert-overview}

\begin{definition}[CompCert]\label{def:compcert}
\emph{CompCert} is a formally verified compiler for a large subset of C, developed by Xavier Leroy and colleagues at INRIA. It is the first commercially usable verified compiler.
\end{definition}

CompCert compiles C to PowerPC, ARM, x86, and RISC-V assembly. Its correctness theorem guarantees that the generated assembly code has the same observable behavior as the source C program, according to the formal semantics of both languages.

\subsection{Architecture}\label{sec:compcert-arch}

CompCert is organized as a chain of verified passes:

