Each pass is individually proved correct. The overall correctness theorem follows by transitivity: if each pass preserves semantics, then the composition of all passes preserves semantics.

\subsection{Intermediate Languages}\label{sec:compcert-languages}

CompCert uses a chain of intermediate languages, each designed to make certain proofs easier:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Clight}: a simplified C with explicit control flow (no \texttt{for}, \texttt{switch}, etc.).
  \item \textbf{Cminor}: a lower-level language with explicit stack frames and no complex expressions.
  \item \textbf{RTL}: a register transfer language with an unbounded number of pseudo-registers and explicit control-flow graph.
  \item \textbf{LTL}: a location-based language with a fixed set of machine registers and stack locations.
  \item \textbf{Macro assembly}: close to the target assembly, but with pseudo-instructions.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Proof Techniques}\label{sec:compcert-techniques}

CompCert's proofs use several techniques:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Forward simulation}: prove that every step of the source can be matched by zero or more steps of the target.
  \item \textbf{Backward simulation}: prove that every step of the target can be matched by zero or more steps of the source.
  \item \textbf{Bisimulation}: prove forward and backward simulation simultaneously.
  \item \textbf{Determination}: prove that the target is deterministic, so forward simulation implies backward simulation.
\end{itemize}

\section{Proof Infrastructure}\label{sec:proof-infra}

\subsection{Coq}\label{sec:coq}

CompCert is developed and proved in the \emph{Coq} proof assistant:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The compiler is implemented as a Coq program (extracted to OCaml for execution).
  \item The correctness proofs are developed in Coq's logic (the Calculus of Inductive Constructions).
  \item The proofs are machine-checked by Coq's kernel.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Proof Effort}\label{sec:proof-effort}

The CompCert project represents a massive proof effort:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Approximately 100,000 lines of Coq proof scripts.
  \item Development over more than 15 years by a team of researchers.
  \item The proof effort is roughly 10--30 times the implementation effort.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Other Verified Compilers}\label{sec:other-verified}

Beyond CompCert, other verified compilers include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Cakeml}: a verified compiler for a functional language (ML-like), proved in HOL4.
  \item \textbf{Pilsner}: a verified compiler for a subset of ML, proved in Isabelle/HOL.
  \item \textbf{Jasmine}: a verified compiler for Java bytecode, proved in Coq.
  \item \textbf{Vellvm}: a verified LLVM framework in Coq.
  \item \textbf{CertiCoq}: a verified compiler from Coq to C, using the Bedrock2 framework.
\end{itemize}

\section{Limitations and Scope of Verification}\label{sec:limitations}

\subsection{What Is Verified}\label{sec:what-verified}

CompCert verifies:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Parsing (from C source to Clight).
  \item All optimization passes (constant propagation, CSE, inlining, register allocation, instruction selection, etc.).
  \item Code generation (from the last IR to assembly).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{What Is Not Verified}\label{sec:what-not-verified}

CompCert does \emph{not} verify:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The C standard library (only the language semantics are verified).
  \item The linker, loader, or operating system.
  \item The hardware (the processor is assumed to match its formal model).
  \item The Coq proof assistant itself (trusted computing base).
  \item The OCaml compiler used to compile the extracted code.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Trusted Computing Base}\label{sec:tcb}

\begin{definition}[Trusted Computing Base]\label{def:tcb}
The \emph{trusted computing base} (TCB) is the set of components that must be correct for the verification to be meaningful. For CompCert, the TCB includes:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The Coq proof assistant (its type checker and kernel).
  \item The formal semantics of C (the CompCert C semantics).
  \item The formal model of the target processor.
  \item The OCaml compiler and runtime.
  \item The hardware and operating system.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Scalability Challenges}\label{sec:scalability}

Verifying a compiler is extremely expensive:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The proof effort is an order of magnitude larger than the implementation effort.
  \item Adding new optimizations requires proving them correct, which slows development.
  \item The verified compiler covers a smaller language subset than unverified compilers.
  \item The performance of verified code may be lower than aggressively optimized unverified code.
\end{itemize}

\section{CompCert: Proof Infrastructure}\label{sec:compcert-proof}

\subsection{Formal Semantics of CompCert C}\label{sec:compcert-semantics}

CompCert's correctness proof rests on a formal semantics of C that carefully handles the language's subtle features:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Defined behavior}: the program has a well-defined outcome (a trace of observable events).
  \item \textbf{Undefined behavior (UB)}: the program can do anything (the semantics imposes no constraints).
  \item \textbf{Implementation-defined behavior}: the behavior is specified by the implementation (e.g., size of \texttt{int}).
  \item \textbf{Volatile}: accesses to volatile variables are observable events.
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[Semantic Preservation for CompCert]\label{def:compcert-preservation}
For a CompCert-compiled program, the target's behaviors are a superset of the source's behaviors:
\[
  \text{behaviors}(T) \supseteq \text{behaviors}(S)
\]
If the source has defined behavior, the target has the same behavior. If the source has undefined behavior, the target can do anything (including having defined behavior---this is the ``optimization from UB'' principle).
\end{definition}

\subsection{Forward and Backward Simulation}\label{sec:simulation-detail}

The proof of each pass uses simulation relations:

\begin{definition}[Forward Simulation]\label{def:forward-sim}
A \emph{forward simulation} from $S$ to $T$ proves that for every execution of $S$, there exists a matching execution of $T$ with the same observable behavior:
\[
  \forall e_S.\; S \to^* e_S \implies \exists e_T.\; T \to^* e_T \land \text{match}(e_S, e_T)
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Backward Simulation]\label{def:backward-sim}
A \emph{backward simulation} from $S$ to $T$ proves that for every execution of $T$, there exists a matching execution of $S$:
\[
  \forall e_T.\; T \to^* e_T \implies \exists e_S.\; S \to^* e_S \land \text{match}(e_S, e_T)
\]
\end{definition}

For deterministic target programs, forward simulation implies backward simulation (and vice versa, given termination). CompCert uses forward simulation for most passes and proves determinism of the target to obtain the full correspondence.

\subsection{Verified Register Allocation}\label{sec:verified-regalloc}

Register allocation in CompCert is verified using a combination of techniques:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Coloring-based allocation}: the allocator uses graph coloring to assign registers.
  \item \textbf{Verification by translation validation}: after allocation, a validator checks that the allocated code is correct (no two conflicting variables share a register, all live ranges are respected).
  \item \textbf{Proof of the validator}: the validator itself is proved correct in Coq.
\end{enumerate}

This approach avoids proving the coloring algorithm correct (which would be extremely complex) while still guaranteeing the correctness of the result.

\section{Proof Techniques for Compiler Passes}\label{sec:proof-techniques}

\subsection{Verifying Constant Propagation}\label{sec:verify-constprop}

Constant propagation replaces variables with known constant values. The proof requires:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Define the abstract domain: $\text{Val} = \{\top\} \cup \{c \mid c \text{ is a constant}\} \cup \{\bot\}$.
  \item Define the transfer function for each instruction.
  \item Prove that the analysis is sound: the abstract values over-approximate the concrete values.
  \item Prove that the transformation preserves semantics: replacing a variable with its abstract value is correct.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{verbatim}
  (* Coq proof sketch for constant propagation correctness *)
  Theorem constprop_correct :
    forall f tf,
      well_formed f ->
      let tf' := constprop_transform f tf in
      forward_simulation (RTL.sem f) (RTL.sem tf').
  
  (* The proof proceeds by:
     1. Showing that the abstract state is a valid approximation
     2. Showing that each transformed instruction simulates the original
     3. Composing the simulations for all instructions *)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Verifying Instruction Selection}\label{sec:verify-instsel}

Instruction selection maps abstract operations to machine instructions. The proof requires showing that each selected instruction sequence computes the same value as the abstract operation:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Define a \emph{matching} relation between abstract operations and instruction sequences.
  \item Prove that each instruction sequence in the matching computes the correct value.
  \item Prove that the instruction selection algorithm only selects matching sequences.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Composing Pass Correctness Proofs}\label{sec:composing-proofs}

The overall correctness theorem is obtained by composing the individual pass theorems:

\begin{theorem}[CompCert Overall Correctness]\label{thm:compcert-overall}
Let $P_1, P_2, \ldots, P_n$ be the compilation passes. If each $P_i$ is proved correct (forward simulation from its input to its output), then:
\[
  \text{forward\_simulation}(\text{C.sem}(S), \text{Asm.sem}(P_n(\cdots P_2(P_1(S))\cdots)))
\]
by transitivity of forward simulation.
\end{theorem}

\section{Scaling Compiler Verification}\label{sec:scaling-verification}

\subsection{Modular Verification}\label{sec:modular-verification}

To scale verification, the compiler is decomposed into independently verified modules:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Each pass has its own correctness theorem.
  \item Passes communicate through well-defined interfaces (intermediate languages).
  \item New passes can be added and verified independently.
  \item The overall theorem follows by composition.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Automation in Compiler Proofs}\label{sec:proof-automation}

Proof automation reduces the manual effort required:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Tactics}: custom Coq tactics for common proof patterns (e.g., proving simulation relations).
  \item \textbf{Reflection}: encoding decision procedures in Coq's logic (e.g., for arithmetic reasoning).
  \item \textbf{SMT integration}: calling external SMT solvers from within Coq proofs (using tools like \texttt{coq-smc}).
  \item \textbf{Code generation}: generating proof scripts from the compiler's implementation.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Verification Challenge for Optimizing Compilers}\label{sec:verification-challenge}

Modern optimizing compilers (GCC, LLVM) perform thousands of transformations. Verifying all of them is currently infeasible. Approaches to scaling include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Verified core}: verify a small, trusted core of passes; use translation validation for the rest.
  \item \textbf{Verified back end}: verify instruction selection, register allocation, and code emission; leave the optimizer unverified.
  \item \textbf{Incremental verification}: verify passes one at a time, building up a verified compiler over years.
  \item \textbf{Community effort}: distribute the verification effort across multiple research groups.
\end{itemize}

\section{Verified Compilation Beyond CompCert}\label{sec:beyond-compcert}

\subsection{The CakeML Verified Compiler}\label{sec:cakeml}

CakeML is a verified compiler for a functional language (a dialect of ML):

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Source language}: a higher-order functional language with pattern matching, references, and I/O.
  \item \textbf{Target}: x86-64 machine code.
  \item \textbf{Proof assistant}: HOL4.
  \item \textbf{Key achievement}: the entire compiler (parser, type checker, code generator) is verified, including a verified runtime (garbage collector).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Cerberus Approach}\label{sec:cerberus}

Cerberus takes a different approach to compiler verification:

\begin{definition}[Cerberus]\label{def:cerberus}
\emph{Cerberus} is a tool that generates test compilers from a formal C semantics. Instead of verifying a specific compiler, it uses the formal semantics as an oracle to check whether a compiler's output is correct.
\end{definition}

\begin{itemize}
  \item The C semantics is executable: given a C program, Cerberus can compute its expected behavior.
  \item Test programs are generated and compiled by the compiler under test.
  \item The compiled program's behavior is compared with Cerberus's expected behavior.
  \item Discrepancies indicate compiler bugs.
\end{itemize}

