\section{Summary}\label{sec:verification-summary}

Compiler verification provides the strongest possible guarantee of compiler correctness: a mathematical proof that the compiled code correctly implements the source program. CompCert demonstrates that verified compilation is practical, but the enormous proof effort and limited scope highlight the challenges. Translation validation offers a more practical alternative, verifying individual compilations without verifying the compiler itself. As verification tools and techniques improve, we can expect verified compilers to become more capable and more widely used.

\begin{historical}
The idea of verifying compilers dates back to the earliest days of formal methods. McCarthy and Painter (1967) described what is arguably the first compiler correctness proof, for a simple arithmetic expression compiler. The modern era of compiler verification began with CompCert (Leroy, 2006), which demonstrated that a realistic, optimizing compiler could be formally verified. The CompCert project has continued to evolve, adding new passes, supporting new targets, and improving performance. The broader field of certified compilation has expanded to include verified type checkers, verified parsers, and verified runtime systems. The interaction between compiler verification and other verification efforts (operating system verification, hardware verification) is an active area of research.
\end{historical}
